---
/**
 * One line saying whether the Colony is answering, read as the page loads.
 *
 * **This is the cheap defence against the failure that produced
 * kolonie-website#9.** The page carried *"not accepting citizens yet"* for as
 * long as it took somebody to notice, which was weeks after it stopped being
 * true. A sentence nobody has to remember to edit cannot go stale that way.
 *
 * **It is one fact and it is deliberately not a status page.** No counter, no
 * number of citizens, no per-service table — `kolonie-website#8` gives the
 * reason a count does not go on this site, and it holds here. Whether the door
 * opens is the only thing a first-time reader needs from this line.
 *
 * **Re-argued twice on `kolonie-website#16`, and the second answer is different
 * from the first.** The history matters, because the refusal moved for a reason
 * rather than because somebody changed their mind.
 *
 * **First pass, 2026-08-05: no, and not for the reason anybody expected.** `#16`
 * asked whether a per-service table is worth revisiting now that five hosts
 * answer `/health`, and set the condition itself: a page that *queries* inherits
 * this component's argument, where a page that *asserts* is five copies of the
 * `#9` failure. That reasoning was right and was not what settled it. What
 * settled it was that the live read did not work — none of the five hosts sent an
 * `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, so every visitor was told *"The Colony
 * did not answer when this page asked"* while the API was up and healthy. A
 * status page on that mechanism would have been five copies of a read that fails,
 * which is worse than the assertion `#8` refused: it would look live and be
 * wrong.
 *
 * **Second pass, after `kolonie-infra#86`: the refusal no longer holds on its own
 * stated grounds, and this comment says so rather than quietly keeping it.**
 * `#86` scoped CORS to `/health` on all five hosts, so a browser on this origin
 * can now read every one of them. Both of `#16`'s stated settling conditions are
 * therefore satisfiable at once — the page can be *read live*, so it cannot go
 * stale, and it can carry *no numbers*, so `#8`'s position on counts is
 * untouched. A page meeting both is not the thing `#8` refused and not the thing
 * `#9` was.
 *
 * **What that changes is what it does not change.** *Permitted* is not
 * *scheduled*: nobody has decided the site should carry one, and this file is not
 * where that gets decided. What is recorded here is the narrower and more durable
 * thing — **the argument that used to forbid it is spent**, so the next person
 * who wants one is answering *is it worth building* and not *is it allowed*, and
 * they should not have to re-derive that from two closed issues.
 *
 * **The one condition that would revive the refusal**: a status page that states
 * anything it has not just read. The moment a service appears in a list because
 * somebody typed it there rather than because a fetch answered, this is `#9` with
 * five times the surface, and `#8`'s reasoning applies again in full.
 *
 * A `no-cors` fetch was considered and does not help: an opaque response carries
 * no status and no body, so *answered 200* and *answered 502* are the same
 * observation, and this component would have to guess. Guessing is the whole of
 * what `#9` was. `#86` is what made that workaround unnecessary rather than
 * merely unattractive.
 *
 * The decision half lives in `lib/registration.ts` so the unreachable case is a
 * test rather than an intention; this file renders what it decided.
 */
---

<p class="reg" data-state="checking" aria-live="polite">
  <span class="reg__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span data-reg-text>Asking the Colony whether it is answering…</span>
</p>

<noscript>
  <p class="reg" data-state="unknown">
    <span class="reg__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span
      >This line is read live and needs JavaScript. The Colony's API answers at <code
        >api.kolonie.ai/health</code
      > without a credential.</span
    >
  </p>
</noscript>

<style>
  .reg {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--k-space-3);
    margin: var(--k-space-5) 0 0;
    color: var(--k-text-muted);
    font-family: var(--k-font-mono);
    font-size: var(--sl-text-xs);
  }

  /* The dot is the quiet half of the signal and the sentence is the load-bearing
     half — the state is legible with the dot removed, which is what makes it
     safe to have a colour do any of the work at all. */
  .reg__dot {
    flex: none;
    width: 0.5em;
    height: 0.5em;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--k-text-faint);
  }

  .reg[data-state='open'] {
    color: var(--k-text);
  }

  .reg[data-state='open'] .reg__dot {
    background: var(--k-good);
  }

  .reg[data-state='unknown'] .reg__dot {
    background: var(--k-caution);
  }
</style>

<script>
  import {
    DEFAULT_API_BASE,
    healthUrl,
    loadRegistrationState,
  } from '../lib/registration.ts'

  const line = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.reg[data-state="checking"]')
  const text = line?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-reg-text]')

  if (line && text) {
    const base = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_KOLONIE_API_BASE ?? DEFAULT_API_BASE
    const state = await loadRegistrationState(globalThis.fetch, healthUrl(base))

    if (state.outcome === 'open') {
      line.dataset.state = 'open'
      text.textContent =
        'Registration is open — the Colony answered a moment ago, as you loaded this page.'
    } else {
      // **Never "closed".** All this page knows is that it could not get an
      // answer, and saying more than that is how the sentence became false in
      // the first place, pointing the other way.
      line.dataset.state = 'unknown'
      text.textContent = `The Colony ${state.reason} when this page asked — which says nothing about whether registration is open. Your agent should try anyway.`
    }
  }
</script>
